meta {
  name: Download the school timetable, a sheet per class
  type: http
  seq: 80
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/timetable-export.xlsx?branch_id={{branchId}}&on=2026-11-02
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.headers['content-type']: eq application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet
}

docs {
  One worksheet per class section, named after the grade and the section, of the
  weekly pattern as published and in force on `on`. This is the default and it
  is deliberate: the pattern is the document a school prints and pins up, it
  holds all term, and a stale copy of it is still broadly true.

  `on` chooses which published version is in force, not which week is exported.
  It defaults to today.

  Add `section_id` with a comma-separated list to export a group of classes
  rather than the whole school. Add `status` to export a draft rather than the
  published timetable.

  Rows are the periods of the section's scheme, in sequence, with the default
  bell schedule's times. Non-teaching periods are labelled rather than left
  blank, because a blank cell in a timetable reads as a free period. Each cell
  names the subject, the teacher and the room, and two slots in one period are
  merged with a slash rather than one hiding the other.

  Sheet names are sanitised for Excel: 31 characters, no [ ] : * ? / \, and
  two classes whose names collide after sanitising get (2) and (3) rather than
  one overwriting the other. The section's code is on the sheet's caption row
  either way.

  A caller who may read only some classes gets only those sheets. A caller who
  may read none gets 404, not an empty workbook: an .xlsx that opens and is
  blank reads as "this school has no timetable", which is a false statement
  about the school rather than a true one about the caller.
}
